+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for hotels in 2026?
+For most teams, the strongest starting stack is `Rasa` or `Langflow` for agent logic, `AnythingLLM` or `Docling` for hotel knowledge retrieval, `Pipecat` or `LiveKit Agents` for voice, and `Ollama` or `vLLM` for private model serving.
+
+### Are there any hotel-specific open source AI tools on GitHub?
+Very few. Direct hotel-only GitHub searches returned weak results, so this list focuses on broader OSS AI repositories that hotels can actually deploy for concierge, guest support, voice, search, OCR, and forecasting.
+
+### Which open source AI tools are best for hotel concierge chatbots?
+`Rasa`, `Flowise`, `Langflow`, `LibreChat`, and `Botpress` are the strongest options here. Pick `Rasa` for control, `Flowise` or `Langflow` for visual orchestration, and `LibreChat` for a polished self-hosted interface.
+
+### What is the best self-hosted AI document stack for hotels?
+`Docling`, `Unstructured`, and `OCRmyPDF` are the strongest document-processing trio, with `AnythingLLM`, `LlamaIndex`, or `Haystack` layered on top for retrieval and conversational access.
+
+### Which open source voice AI tools work well for hotels?
+For realtime assistants, use `Pipecat` or `LiveKit Agents`. Pair them with `faster-whisper` for transcription and `Piper` or `Coqui TTS` for speech output depending on whether you prefer stable offline deployment or more customizable TTS workflows.
+
+### What open source AI tools help hotels with pricing and demand forecasting?
+`NeuralForecast`, `StatsForecast`, `MLForecast`, `HierarchicalForecast`, and `PyCaret` are the best fits in this list. They help with occupancy, ADR, channel demand, staffing expectations, and multi-property reconciliation.
+
+### Which repos should hotel teams avoid if they want true open source?
+Be careful with products that are only source-available, strongly cloud-tied, or licensed with non-standard restrictions. This list intentionally filtered out several popular AI repos when the “open source for hotels” claim felt too weak.
+
